\h Ezra
\toc1 Ezra
\toc2 Ezra
\mt1 Ezra
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Cyrus returns some Jews
\p
\v 1 In the first year that Cyrus was king of Persia, Yahweh stirred up his spirit in order to fulfil what he’d spoken through \add the prophet\add* Jeremiah. So the king distributed an \add official\add* written notice throughout his kingdom, saying,
\v 2 “Cyrus, king of Persia, declares this: Yahweh, the god of heaven, has given me all the kingdoms in the world, and he’s appointed me to build a residence for him in Jerusalem in Judah.
\v 3 Anyone among you who’s one of his followers is free to go to Jerusalem (in Judah) \add to help\add* build a temple there for Israel’s god Yahweh—the god of Jerusalem. May God go with you.
\v 4 Those who live near these people but who are not going themselves, should donate gold and silver, and goods and livestock, plus cash as a voluntary offering for God’s temple in Jerusalem.
\p
\v 5 Then \add some\add* of the leaders of Benjamin and Judah, and \add some\add* priests and Levites—all of whom had had their spirits stirred up by God—left to go and build Yahweh’s residence in Jerusalem.
\v 6 Their neighbours had helped them by donating gold and silver utensils, goods and livestock, and expensive gifts, as well as the voluntary offerings of cash.
\p
\v 7 Then King Cyrus brought out the equipment from Yahweh’s temple that Nebuchadnezzar had brought \add all the way\add* from Jerusalem and had put in the house of his gods.
\v 8 He put the treasurer Mitredat in charge of bringing them out and Sheshebatsar, the ruler of Judah, in charge of accepting them and listing them.
\v 9 There were: thirty gold basins, one thousand silver basins, twenty-nine knives,
\v 10 thirty gold bowls, 410 silver bowls, and a thousand other utensils,
\v 11 coming to a total of 5,400 gold and silver utensils. Sheshebatsar took all of that with the group of exiles going from Babylon to Jerusalem.
\c 2
\s1 The list of returning exiles
\r (Neh. 7:4-73)
\p
\v 1 Out of the captives that Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar had brought \add as slaves\add* to Babylon, these are \add their descendants\add* who returned to Jerusalem in Judah—each person returning to their own ancestral town.
\v 2 The ones who went with Zerubbabel were: Yeshua, Nehemiah, Serayah, Re’elayah, Mordecai, Bilshan, Mispar, Bigvay, Rehum, and Ba’anah.
\p Their numbers were:
\li1
\v 3 2,172 descendants of Par’osh,
\li1
\v 4 372 descendants of Shefatyah,
\li1
\v 5 775 descendants of Arah,
\li1
\v 6 2,812 descendants of Pahat-Moab, from the families of Yeshua and Joab,
\li1
\v 7 1,254 descendants of Eylam,
\li1
\v 8 945 descendants of Zatu,
\li1
\v 9 760 descendants of Zakay,
\li1
\v 10 642 descendants of Bani,
\li1
\v 11 623 descendants of Bevay,
\li1
\v 12 1,222 descendants of Azgad,
\li1
\v 13 666 descendants of Adonikam,
\li1
\v 14 2,056 descendants of Bigvay,
\li1
\v 15 454 descendants of Adin,
\li1
\v 16 98 descendants of Ater, \add who descended\add* from Hezekiah,
\li1
\v 17 323 descendants of Betsay,
\li1
\v 18 112 descendants of Yorah,
\li1
\v 19 223 descendants of Hashum,
\li1
\v 20 95 descendants of Givar.
\p
\v 21 \add And from these towns:\add*
\li1 123 from Bethlehem,
\li1
\v 22 56 from Netofah,
\li1
\v 23 128 from Anatot,
\li1
\v 24 42 from Azmavet,
\li1
\v 25 743 from Kiryat-Yearim, Kefirah, and Be’erot,
\li1
\v 26 621 from Ramah and Geva,
\li1
\v 27 122 from Mikmas,
\li1
\v 28 223 from Bethel and Ai,
\li1
\v 29 52 from Nevo,
\li1
\v 30 156 from Magbish,
\li1
\v 31 1,254 from the other Eylam,
\li1
\v 32 320 from Harim,
\li1
\v 33 725 from Lod, Hadid, and Ono,
\li1
\v 34 345 from Jericho,
\li1
\v 35 3,630 from Sena’ah.
\p
\v 36 The priests \add who returned\add*:
\li1 973 descendants of Yedayah (descendants of Yeshua),
\li1
\v 37 1,052 descendants of Imer,
\li1
\v 38 1,247 descendants of Pashhur,
\li1
\v 39 1,017 descendants of Harim.
\p
\v 40 The Levites \add who returned\add*:
\li1 74 descendants of Yeshua and Kadmiel, who were from the family of Hodavyah,
\li1
\v 41 128 musicians who were descendants of Asaf,
\li1
\v 42 139 gatekeepers who were descendants of the gatekeepers Shalum, Ater, Talmon, Akuv, Hatita, and Shovay,
\p
\v 43 The temple servants \add who returned\add*:
\li1 The descendants of Tsiha, Hasufa, and Tabaeot,
\li1
\v 44 the descendants of Keyros, Siea, and Padon,
\li1
\v 45 the descendants of Levana, Hagava, and Akuv,
\li1
\v 46 the descendants of Hagav, Shalmay, and Hanan,
\li1
\v 47 the descendants of Gidel, Gahar, and Reayah,
\li1
\v 48 the descendants of Retsin, Nekoda, and Gazam,
\li1
\v 49 the descendants of Uza, Paseah, and Besay,
\li1
\v 50 the descendants of Asnah, Meunim, and Nefushesim,
\li1
\v 51 the descendants of Bakbuk, Hakufa, and Harhur,
\li1
\v 52 the descendants of Batslut, Mehida, and Harsha,
\li1
\v 53 the descendants of Barkos, Sisera, and Temah,
\li1
\v 54 and the descendants of Netsiah and Hatifa.
\p
\v 55 The descendants of Solomon’s servants \add who returned\add*:
\li1 the descendants Sotay, Soferet, Peruda,
\li1
\v 56 the descendants of Ya’alah, Darkon, and Gidel,
\li1
\v 57 the descendants of Shefatyah, Hatil, Pokeret Tsevayim, and Ami.
\m
\v 58 \add Altogether\add* there were 392 descendants of temple workers and Solomon’s servants \add who returned\add*.
\p
\v 59 Another group went from \add the towns of\add* Tel-Melah, Tel-Harsha, Keruv, Adan, and Imer, but they didn’t know their ancestry \add from before they were taken as captives\add*.
\p
\v 60 There were 652 people who were descendants of Delayah, Toviyah, and Nekoda,
\v 61 and from the sons of the priests: the descendants of Havayah; the descendants of Hakots; and the descendants of Barzilay, who took a wife from the daughters of Barzilay the Giladi, so he was called by their name.
\v 62 They had searched for their records among the genealogies, but couldn’t find \add their families listed\add*, so they were disqualified to serve as priests.
\v 63 Also the governor told them that they mustn’t eat any of the holiest food until a priest could use the Urim and Tumim \add to determine their status\add*.
\p
\v 64 Altogether in that group, 42,360 people \add returned to Judah\add*.
\v 65 not counting their 7,337 male and female servants, plus 200 male and female musicians.
\v 66 \add They also took\add* 736 horses and 245 mules,
\v 67 435 camels and 6,720 donkeys.
\p
\v 68 When some of the heads of families got to Yahweh’s temple in Jerusalem, they freely donated so that it could be rebuilt.
\v 69 They each donated to the treasury according to their individual resources—\add a total of\add* sixty-one thousand gold coins, five thousand silver bars, and a hundred sets of clothing for the priests.
\p
\v 70 So the priests and the Levites, and some of the people, and the singers, and the gatekeepers and the temple servants \add returned to\add* live in their \add ancestral\add* cities, and \add so\add* all the Israelite cities had returnees living in them.
\c 3
\s1 Rebuilding the altar
\p
\v 1 In the seventh month, the Israelites from their \add various\add* cities, gathered together in unity in Jerusalem.
\v 2 Then Yotsadak’s son Yeshua and his brothers the priests, and Shealtiel’s son Zerubbabel and his brothers, and they built the altar to offer up burnt offerings to Israel’s god on it as is written in the law of Moses, the man of God.
\v 3 They set the altar up on its foundation because they were worried \add about trouble from\add* some of the \add other\add* people groups in the region. Then they offered burnt offerings to Yahweh on it \add as well as the regular\add* morning and evening burnt offerings.
\v 4 Then they observed the Celebration in Shelters as per the written instructions with a burnt offering each day as required.
\v 5 After that \add was over\add*, they kept up the regular burnt offerings, plus those for the new moons and other times that Yahweh required, as well as any free-will offerings to Yahweh.
\v 6 Even though the temple foundation rebuilding hadn’t started yet, the people started sacrificing burnt offerings to Yahweh from the beginning of October.
\s1 Rebuilding the temple
\p
\v 7 They donated cash to the stone-workers and carpenters, and food, drink, and oil to the workers from Sidon and Tyre to float cedar \add logs along the coast\add* from Lebanon into Joppa port with the permission of Persian King Cyrus.
\p
\v 8 In the second month of second year since their return to Jerusalem, Shealtiel’s son Zerubbabel and Yotsadak’s son Yeshua, and the rest of their brothers the priests and the Levites, and everyone except who’d returned to Jerusalem from captivity, began work on God’s residence, and they appointed the Levites who were twenty and over to act as overseers for the work of \add rebuilding\add* Yahweh’s temple.
\v 9 And Yeshua with his brothers and their sons, Kadmiel and his sons, (all descendants of Judah cooperated to act as overseers for those doing the work at the temple \add together with\add* Henadad’s sons and grandsons, and their brothers the Levites.
\p
\v 10 When the builders had laid the foundation of Yahweh’s temple, they got the priests to stand there in their robes with their trumpets, and Asaf’s descendants (who were Levites) with their cymbals, to praise Yahweh using the rhythms composed by David, the \add former\add* Israelite king.
\v 11 Then they sang in response—praising and thanking Yahweh: “Yes, he is good, because his loyal commitment to Israel will continue forever.”
\p Then all the people shouted a great shout in praise to Yahweh because the foundation of Yahweh’s \add temple\add* had been laid.
\v 12 Many of the older priests and Levites, and clan leaders who’d seen the first temple, wept loudly when they witnessed the founding of this one, but others were shouting happily with loud voices.
\v 13 So the people couldn’t differentiate the happy sounds from the sound of the people weeping, because the people were shouting very loudly and it could be heard from far away.
\c 4
\s1 Opposition to rebuilding the temple
\p
\v 1 Now the enemies of Judah and Benjamin heard that the Israelites who’d come back from exile were building a temple for Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 2 so they went to Zerubbabel and to the \add other\add* clan leaders and they said to them, “Let us help you with the building work, because like you, we worship your god and we’ve been sacrificing to him since the days of the Assyrian king Esar-Hadon—the one who sent us here.”
\p
\v 3 But Zerubbabel, and Yeshua, and the rest of the Israelites clan leaders said to them, “It’s not for you and for us to build a house for our god, but we ourselves together will build for Israel’s god Yahweh, just as the Persian King Cyrus has commanded us.”
\p
\v 4 Those local people had been discouraging the people of Judah and trying to stop them from building.
\v 5 They’d \add also\add* hired influencers to work against them throughout the reigns of the Persian kings Cyrus and Darius.
\s1 The opposition of replacement of Jerusalem
\p
\v 6 Now at the beginning of the reign of Ahasuerus, they forwarded an accusation against those who lived in Jerusalem and \add throughout\add* Judah.
\p
\v 7 And in the days of Persian King Artaxerxes, Bishlam, Mitredat, Teve’el and the rest of their companions wrote to the king. (The letter was written in Syrian and using that alphabet).
\p
\v 8 Rehum the high commissioner and Shimshay the provincial secretary wrote a letter to King Artaxerxes against Jerusalem as follows:
\p
\v 9 \add From\add* Rehum the high commissioner and Shimshay the provincial secretary, and the our companions, the judges and the rulers, the officials, the Persians, the Erekites, the Babylonians, the Susaites (that is, the Elamites),
\v 10 and the rest of the nations that the famous and powerful \add King\add* Asenapar exiled \add from other nations\add* and forced them to live in the cities of Samaria, and the province went of the Euphrates river.
\p And now
\v 11 this is what the letter said that they sent to him:
\p To Artaxerxes the king \add from\add* your servants on this side of the river.
\p And now
\v 12 let it be known to the king that the Jews who left your region have come to us at Jerusalem to rebuild that rebellious and evil city—they’re \add currently\add* finishing the walls and repairing the \add building\add* foundations.
\v 13 Now let it be known to the king that if that city is built and the walls are completed, those people won’t pay taxes, \add or send\add* tributes, and your royal treasury will lose out.
\v 14 Now, because we’ve had the favour of the palace, and because we don’t want to see the king dishonoured, that’s why we’ve sent this letter to update the king,
\v 15 so that he can search in the official records of the kingdom. You’ll discover and learn that that city \add of Jerusalem\add* is a rebellious city and one that has caused harm to kings and provinces, and has harboured rebellion there for decades past—that’s why that city was destroyed.
\v 16 We are letting the king know that if that city is rebuilt and its walls are completed, then you’ll lose control of this province on this side of the Euphrates.”
\p
\v 17 The king sent this reply:
\p “To Rehum the high commissioner and Shimshay the provincial secretary, and the rest of their companions who live in Samaria, and the rest of the people in the province west of the Euphrates: Peace.
\p And now
\v 18 the letter that you all sent to us has been carefully read aloud to me.
\v 19 I ordered my officials to search \add the records\add* and discovered \add that it’s true\add* that that city has been rebellious and seditious since ancient times.
\v 20 Powerful kings have reigned from Jerusalem and ruled the entire region that side of the river, and had taxes and tributes paid to them.
\v 21 So command those Jews to stop rebuilding the city until such time as I make a decree.
\v 22 Don’t be negligent in doing that or the damage to this kingdom might get worse.”
\p
\v 23 When King Artaxerxes’ letter \add reached\add* Rehum, Shimshay the secretary, and their companions, and was read aloud to them all, they immediately went to Jerusalem and used force to stop the Jews \add from their rebuilding\add*.
\p
\v 24 So there was a period when the rebuilding of the temple in Jerusalem stopped, and it remained paused until the second year of King Darius’ reign in Persia.
\c 5
\s1 The temple rebuilding continues
\p
\v 1 Then the prophet Haggai and Ido’s son Zechariah prophesied to the Jews in Jerusalem and across Judah in the name of Israel’s god who they served.
\v 2 Then Shealtiel’s son Zerubbabel and Yotsadak’s son Yeshua took action and began to rebuild God’s temple in Jerusalem, and the prophets who served God were \add there\add* with them, supporting them.
\p
\v 3 At that time, Tatenay, the governor of the province west of the Euphrates, and Shetar-Bozenay, and their companions came to them and demanded, “Who gave you all permission to rebuild this temple?”
\v 4 They also asked the Jews to tell them the names of the men who were working on the building.
\v 5 However, God was watching over the Jewish leaders, and they weren’t \add actually\add* stopped. A report was sent to \add King\add* Darius, then they waited for a response.
\v 6 Tatenay, the governor of the province west of the river, and Shetar-Bozenay and his companions, the provincial officials, sent the letter to King Darius—
\v 7 this is what was written in their report:
\p “To King Darius. All peace.
\v 8 Let it be known to the king that we went to Judea province, to the temple of the great God, and it is being built with large stones, and timber is being placed in the walls. This work is being done to a high standard and they’re making good progress.
\p
\v 9 “Then we asked those elders, ‘Who gave you all permission to rebuild this temple?’
\v 10 We also asked them their names, so that we could write down the names of their leaders to inform you.
\p
\v 11 “This is the answer they gave us, ‘We are servants of the god of the heavens and earth, and we are rebuilding the temple that was constructed and finished many years ago by a famous Israelite king.
\v 12 However, because our ancestors made the god of the heavens angry, he let Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar \add Nebuchadnezzar,\add* the Chaldean) defeat them, and he destroyed that temple and caused the people to be exiled to Babylon.
\v 13 But in the first year \add of the reign of\add* Babylonian King Cyrus, he made a decree to rebuild this house of God.
\v 14 What’s more, the gold and silver containers that Nebuchadnezzar had taken out from that temple that was in Jerusalem and had brought to the temple in Babylon, King Cyrus took them out from the temple in Babylon and they were handed over to the man named Sheshebatsar who he’d appointed as governor \add over Judea\add*.
\v 15 Cyrus had told him to take those containers and put them back in the temple in Jerusalem, and to ensure that the temple got rebuilt in the same place where it had been before.
\v 16 So Sheshebatsar came \add here\add* and laid the foundation of God’s house in Jerusalem, and since then, it’s been being rebuilt but isn’t finished yet.’
\p
\v 17 So now, if it pleases the king, let a search be made in \add the records in\add* the king’s treasure house there in Babylon, if it’s \add correct\add* that a decree was set by King Cyrus to rebuild this house of God in Jerusalem. Then let the king send his decision on this matter back to us.”
\c 6
\s1 The former approval is found
\p
\v 1 So King Darius ordered that a search be conducted in \add all\add* the archives where treasures had been deposited there in Babylon,
\v 2 and one scroll was found in the fortress at Ahmeta in Media province, and this record had been made:
\p
\v 3 “In year one of Cyrus the king, he made a decree about the house of God in Jerusalem:
\m ‘Let the house be built in the \add same\add* place where sacrifices were made. Establish its foundations and build it 27m high and 27m wide
\v 4 with three layers of large stones and a layer of new timber. That should all be paid for from the royal treasury.
\v 5 Also, the gold and silver containers from the house of God that Nebuchadnezzar had taken out from the Jerusalem temple and had brought to Babylon, must be returned to their places in the temple in Jerusalem. So you must put them in the house of God.’ ”
\s1 King Darius orders resumption of work
\p
\v 6 \add Therefore King Darius\add* \add wrote:\add* “Now to Governor Tatenay in the province west of the Euphrates, Shetar-Bozenay, and their companions, the officials who in that province:
\p Keep away from that place.
\v 7 Leave alone the work on that house of God. Let the Jewish governor and elders build that temple in its place.
\v 8 So here’s my decree about what should be done for those Jewish elders to build that house of God: Using the king’s treasures that come from the tribute of that west-Euphrates province, let those workers costs be regularly reimbursed so that the work doesn’t stop.
\v 9 Whatever is needed (including young bulls, or rams, or lambs for burnt offerings to the god of the heavens, wheat, salt, wine, or oil, according to the command of the priests in Jerusalem, let it be given to them day by day (that is, without delay),
\v 10 so that they can be offering sweet-smelling sacrifices to the god of the heavens and praying for the life of the king and his sons.
\v 11 Also I decree that any man who changes this edict should have a beam pulled from his house, and \add after it’s sharpened and\add* set into the ground, then he should be impaled on it. Then his \add entire\add* house should be made into a rubbish heap because of what he did.
\v 12 May the god who has established his name there overthrow any king or person who makes any attempt to change \add this decree or\add* to destroy that house of God in Jerusalem. I, Darius, have made a decree. Let it be done diligently.”
\s1 The temple dedication
\p
\v 13 Then Tatenay, the governor of the region west of the Euphrates, Shetar-Bozenay, and their companions worked hard to follow the instructions sent by King Darius.
\v 14 So the Jewish elders continued building, and they were encouraged by the prophesying of the prophet Haggai and Ido’s son Zechariah. As a result of the decrees of the Persian kings Cyrus , and Darius and Artaxerxes, they were able to fulfil God’s decree to rebuild \add the temple\add*.
\v 15 The \add rebuilt\add* temple was finished in mid-March in the sixth year of King Darius’s reign.
\p
\v 16 Then the Israelite people, the priests, and the Levites, and the ones that had returned from exile, performed the dedication of this house of God with celebrations.
\v 17 \add During\add* the dedication, they offered a hundred bulls, two-hundred rams, and four-hundred lambs, as well as twelve male goats for a sin offering for all Israel matching the number of the tribes).
\v 18 Then they organised the priests to stand in their divisions and the Levites in their sections to serve the god who resides in Jerusalem, according to the instructions written by Moses.
\s1 The Feast of ‘pass-over’ of Messenger
\p
\v 19 So the people who’d returned from exile celebrated the ‘pass-over’ in late April.
\v 20 The priests and Levites all had to be individually purified, and they slaughtered the lambs for all the exiles and for their brothers the priests, and for themselves.
\v 21 Then all the Israelites ate \add the meal\add*—those who’d returned from the exile and every one who’d separated themselves from the uncleanness of the nations of the land in order to search for Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 22 Then they happily enjoyed the Flat Bread Celebration for seven days, because Yahweh had made them happy, and had caused the Assyrian king to show favour to them, to assist their work rebuilding the house of God, the god of Israel.
\c 7
\s1 Ezra arrives in Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 Many years later in the reign of Persian King Artaxerxes, Ezra (son of Serayah, son of Azaryah, son of Hilkiyah,
\v 2 son of Shalum, son of Zadok, son of Ahituv,
\v 3 son of Amaryah, son of Azaryah, son of Merayot,
\v 4 son of Zerahyah, son of Uzi, son of Buki,
\v 5 son of Avishua, the son of Pinhas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron the head priest)
\v 6 was a skilled expert in the law that Israel’s god Yahweh had given through Moses. With the favour of his god Yahweh helping Ezra, the king had granted all his requests, and he travelled from Babylon to Jerusalem.
\v 7 Some Israelites, including some priests and Levites, singers and gatekeepers, and some temple servants travelled to Jerusalem in the seventh year of Artaxerxes’s reign.
\v 8-9 They left Babylon on New Year’s Day \add (in early April)\add* and with God’s favour \add on the journey\add*, arrived in Jerusalem exactly four months later
\v 10 because Ezra had made a firm resolution to study Yahweh’s \add written\add* instructions, and to \add carefully\add* follow them, and to teach them in Israel.
\s1 King Artaxerxes’s letter to Ezra
\p
\v 11 King Artaxerxes had given a letter for Ezra the priest and scribe and expert in Yahweh’s commands and instructions \add to take with him\add*, saying:
\p
\v 12 Artaxerxes, the king of kings, to the priest Ezra, the scribe of the law of the god of heavens: Peace.
\p Now,
\v 13 I hereby decree that anyone in my kingdom from Israel including priests and Levites) who freely offers to go to Jerusalem with you, may go.
\v 14 You are sent by the king and his seven counsellors to determine whether \add the people of\add* Judah and Jerusalem are following the law of your god which you have a copy of.
\v 15 You are also to take the gold and silver that the king and his counsellors have freely offered to the god of Israel who lives in Jerusalem,
\v 16 along with all the gold and silver that you collect all across Babylon, along with the freewill offerings of the people and the priests who freely give towards the house of God in Jerusalem.
\p
\v 17 So then you should diligently use those funds to buy bulls, rams, lambs, and \add supplies for\add* their grain offerings and drink offerings, and you should offer them on the altar at your god’s house in Jerusalem.
\v 18 With the remaining funds, you can use them for whatever seems good to you and your brothers, according to the will of your god.
\v 19 Also, make sure that you deliver all those \add gold and silver\add* containers that were handed over to you for use in your god’s house in Jerusalem.
\v 20 Anything else that’s needed for the house of your god that you need to supply can be provided from the king’s treasuries.
\p
\v 21 And I, King Artaxerxes make a decree for all the treasurers in the province west of the river: that you all diligently help with everything that the priest Ezra (the scribe of the law of the god of the heavens) asks from you all—
\v 22 up to three tonnes of silver, five hundred bushels of wheat, two thousand litres of wine and the same amount of \add olive\add* oil, and as much salt as he needs.
\v 23 Everything that the god of the heavens decreed to be done should be done exactly as he specified because there’s no good reason to make him angry at the kingdom of the king and his sons?
\v 24 Also, it should be made clear that there is no authority to extract taxes or tribute from any priests or Levites, singers or the gatekeepers, the temple servants or the servants of that house of God.
\p
\v 25 As for you, Ezra, use the wisdom that your god has given to you to appoint magistrates and judges who can judge all the people who are in \add that province\add* beyond the river—everyone who know the laws of your god (plus you should teach those who don’t know them).
\v 26 Anyone who refuses to obey your god’s laws and the king’s laws, should receive swift judgement—whether it’s confiscation of goods, or banishment, imprisonment, or the death sentence.”
\s1 Ezra praises Yahweh
\p
\v 27 Blessed be Yahweh, the god of our fathers who put it into the king’s heart to honour Yahweh’s house in Jerusalem,
\v 28 and showed me his loyal commitment in dealing with the king and his counsellors, and all the powerful officials of the king. And as for me, Yahweh my god blessed me with \add initiative and\add* the strength to carry it out as I gathered some Israelite leaders to go to Jerusalem with me.
\c 8
\s1 The returning exiles
\p
\v 1 Now these are the family heads and their descendants by number who went with me from Babylon to Jerusalem in the reign of King Artaxerxes:
\li1
\v 2 from the descendants of Pinhas: Gereshom,
\li1 from the descendants of Itamar: Daniel,
\li1 from the descendants of David: Hatush,
\li1
\v 3 from the descendants of Shekanyah and Par’osh: Zechariah and a hundred and fifty \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 4 from the descendants of Pahat-Moab: Zerahyah’s son Elyehoeynay and two hundred \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 5 from the descendants of Shekanyah: Yahaziel’s son and three hundred \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 6 from the descendants of Adin: Jonathan’s son Eved and fifty \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 7 from the descendants of Eylam: Athaliah’s son Isaiah and seventy \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 8 from the descendants of Shefatyah: Michael’s son Zevadyah and eighty \add other\add* men;
\li1
\v 9 from the descendants of Joab: Yehiel’s son Obadiah and 218 \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 10 from the descendants of Shelomit: Yosifyah’s son and one hundred and sixty \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 11 from the descendants of Bevay: his son Zechariah and twenty-eight \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 12 from the descendants of Azgad: Hakatan’s son Yohanan and one hundred and ten \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 13 from the later descendants of Adonikam: Elifelet, Yeiel, and Shemayah, and sixty \add other\add* men,
\li1
\v 14 and from the descendants of Bigvay: Utay and Zakur and seventy \add other\add* men.
\s1 Ezra finds some Levites to take
\p
\v 15 \add Firstly,\add* I gathered them near the river that goes to Ahava, and we camped there for three days. I interviewed the people and the priests and discovered that we didn’t have any Levites included \add in the group\add*.
\v 16 So I summoned the following leaders: Eliezer, Ariel, Shemayah, Elnatan, Yariv, \add a second\add* Elnatan, Nathan, Zechariah, and Meshulam, as well as the wise men: Yoyariv and \add a third\add* Elnatan.
\v 17 I commanded them to go to Ido, the leader at Kasifya, and I told them to tell Ido and his relatives (temple servants in Kasifya) to send us some Levites who would \add go to Jerusalem\add* \add to\add* serve in our god’s temple.
\v 18 Then since we had God’s favour, they sent us a talented man (from the sons of Mahli, son of Levi, son of Israel and eighteen of his sons and other relatives.
\v 19 \add They also sent\add* Hashavyah and Isaiah from Merari’s sons), and their relatives and sons—some twenty men—
\v 20 plus two hundred and twenty \add descendants of\add* Levite temple servants that \add King\add* David and his officials had appointed for service—all designated by name.
\s1 Ezra’s fasting and praying
\p
\v 21 Then \add right\add* there by the river Ahava, I declared a fast to humble ourselves in front of our god to request that he’d smooth the way \add ahead\add* for us, and for our children, and for all our property,
\v 22 because I was ashamed to request an army and horsemen from the king to help us from possible enemies on the way. Also, you see, we had \add previously\add* told the king, “Our god does good to all those who honour him, but his might and his anger are against all those who abandon him.”
\v 23 So we fasted and asked for \add wisdom\add* from our god concerning that, and he listened to our requests.
\s1 The gift and donations for the temple
\p
\v 24 Then I chose twelve of the leaders of the priests along with Shereveyah and Hashavyah and ten of the other Levites,
\v 25 and \add as they watched,\add* I weighed out the gold and silver, and the \add valuable\add* utensils—the offering for the house of our god that the king, and his counsellors and officials, and all the exiled Israelites who could be contacted, had offered.
\v 26 It came to three tonnes of gold, twenty-one tonnes of silver, and another three tonnes of silver containers,
\v 27 as well as twenty gold bowls that weighed another eight kilograms, and two polished bronze containers that were as valuable as gold.
\p
\v 28 “You are sacred to Yahweh,” I told them all, “and the containers and utensils are sacred, and the gold and silver are a freewill offering to Yahweh, the god of your ancestors.
\v 29 Look after them all carefully until you weigh them out in front of the leaders of the priests and Levites and the leaders of the Israelite elders in the chambers of Yahweh’s house in Jerusalem.”
\v 30 So the priests and the Levites accepted the receipt of the gold and silver, and the containers, to take them to Jerusalem—to the house of our god.
\s1 The return to Jerusalem
\p
\v 31 Then in mid-April, we set out from the river Ahava to go to Jerusalem, and we had God’s favour as he kept us safe from any enemies or ambushes on the way.
\v 32 So we arrived at Jerusalem, and rested there for three days.
\v 33 On the fourth day, the gold and silver and the containers and utensils were weighed out in the temple—being handed over to Uriah’s son Meremot, the priest, and with him was Pinhas’s son Eleazar (and with them were Yeshua’s son Yozavad, and Binuy’s son Noadyah, the Levites).
\v 34 They recorded all the items with their weights.
\p
\v 35 The descendants of the former captives who had returned from exile offered burnt offerings to Israel’s god: twelve bulls for all Israel, 96 rams, 77 lambs, then twelve male goats for a sin offering. The whole was \add presented as\add* a burnt offering to Yahweh.
\v 36 They relayed the king’s orders to the governors and officials of \add the province\add* beyond the river, and so they honoured the people and God’s house.
\c 9
\s1 Ezra’s reaction against mixed marriages
\p
\v 1 Now as soon as those things were finished, the leaders came to me, saying, “The people of Israel, and the priests and Levites haven’t kept themselves separate from the idolatry of the peoples of the lands: the Canaanites , Hittites, Perizim, the Jebusites, Ammonites, Moabites, Egyptians , and the Amorites.
\v 2 Instead, they’ve taken women from those peoples as wives for themselves and their sons, so they’ve diluted their sacred heritage with the anti-God customs of those peoples. But the worst is that it’s our leaders and officials that have been at the forefront in this unfaithfulness.”
\v 3 As soon as I heard that, I tore my clothes and pulled some of the hair out of my head and beard, and I sat down appalled.
\v 4 Any others who respectfully followed Israel’s god’s instructions \add and who were concerned\add* about the unfaithfulness of the exiles, joined with me and I sat there appalled until \add the time of\add* the evening sacrifice.
\s1 Ezra’s prayer
\p
\v 5 So I got up from my \add position of\add* humiliation with my torn clothes \add during\add* the evening sacrifice, then I went down on my knees and spread out my palms to my god Yahweh
\v 6 and prayed, “My god, I’m ashamed and humiliated to raise my face to you, my god, because our sins have multiplied \add and risen\add* above our heads, and our guilt has mounted up as high as the sky.
\v 7 From \add back in\add* the time of our ancestors until today, we’ve been very guilty, and in our disobedience, we ourselves, \add including\add* our kings and our priests, have been killed by the kings of the surrounding countries, or taken captive, or been plundered, or simply put to shame, which is the case right now.
\v 8 But now for a brief moment, favour has come from our god Yahweh \add and given\add* us remaining survivors a stake in his holy place—\add yes,\add* our god has cheered us up and given us a little relief from our slavery.
\v 9 Because we are slaves, yet our god hasn’t abandoned us in our slavery, but he’s used his loyal commitment \add to us\add* to influence the Persian king to give to us breathing space to rebuild the house of our god from its ruins, and to give to us a ‘wall \add of protection\add*’ in Jerusalem and across Judah.
\p
\v 10 “So now, our god, what can we say after that? We’ve abandoned your instructions
\v 11 which you gave us via your servants, the prophets, saying, ‘The land that you’re all entering to occupy is a land of impurity, due to the impurity of the current inhabitants with their evil practices that have filled it from one end to the other with their filth.
\v 12 So now, don’t give your daughters to their sons \add to marry\add*, or vice versa. And don’t try to make long-term peace treaties or contracts with them. Then you all will stay in power, and eat the good things of the land, and be able to pass it on to your children for all their future generations.’
\p
\v 13 “Yet after everything that’s happened to us as a result our evil actions and our great guilt (even though you, our god, didn’t punish us as harshly as you could have, and you’ve allowed us to survive as we’re here now),
\v 14 should we return to disobeying your instructions and to intermarrying with those people groups with their evil practices? You’d \add rightfully\add* be angry \add enough\add* with us as to wipe us out completely without leaving any remnant or survivors?
\v 15 Yahweh, the god of Israel, you always do what is right, because \add here\add* we are as survivors even to this day. So, yes, here we are standing in front of you in our guilt, because none of us are innocent enough to stand in front of you because of what we’ve done.”
\c 10
\s1 The people’s acceptance of their sin
\p
\v 1 When Ezra prayed and confessed \add like that\add*, weeping and bowing down low in front of the temple, an extremely large number of Israelite men and women and children gathered around him to join in and weep aloud.
\v 2 Then Yehiel’s son Shekanyah (from the sons of Eylam) answered and admitted to Ezra, “We ourselves have behaved unfaithfully towards our god and have married foreign women from the \add other\add* people groups in this region. But there’s still hope for Israel concerning this.
\v 3 So now, let’s make an agreement with our god to send all those women back, along with their children, following the advice of my master and the ones who strive to obey God’s instructions, and as per the law.
\v 4 So take action because this is now in your hands, but we’re with you. Lead strongly and make a clear decision.”
\p
\v 5 So Ezra took action and made the leaders of the priests and Levites, and all Israel, to promise to implement that decision, and they did so.
\v 6 Then Ezra left that place there in front of the temple, and he went to Yehohanan’s room (Elyashiv’s son) and went in. He didn’t eat or drink, but continued mourning because of the unfaithfulness of the exiles.
\p
\v 7 Then the leaders sent an official written notice throughout Jerusalem and all Judah to all those Jews who’d returned from captivity, that their men should assemble in Jerusalem.
\v 8 \add They were told that\add* it was the decision of the leaders and elders that anyone who didn’t arrive within three days would then forfeit all their property and would no longer be considered as an Israelite.
\v 9 So on the third day (in mid-December), all the men of Judah and Benjamin gathered in Jerusalem, and all the people sat in the courtyard in front of the temple. They were shivering because of the rain, and because of \add the seriousness\add* of the matter.
\p
\v 10 Then the priest Ezra stood up and announced, “You yourselves have acted unfaithfully and have married foreign women, thus adding to Israel’s guilt \add in God’s eyes\add*.
\v 11 Now however, praise Yahweh, the god of your ancestors, and do what he’s commanded us and separate yourselves from \add the other religions of\add* the peoples in this region, including not marrying their women.”
\p
\v 12 Then all the gathered Israelites answered, “Yes, we do need to do what you’ve said.
\v 13 However, it’s the rainy season now, and there’s so many people who can’t stand outside for several days while we try to work all this out, because we’ve done so much wrong.
\v 14 So let’s delegate our leaders to make the decisions for all of us. Then we can arrange to meet together by city with our elders and judges to apply the decisions about our foreign wives, until God’s anger towards us about these matters cools down.
\v 15 The only ones who were against that \add plan\add* were Asahel’s on Jonathan and Tikvah’s son Yahzeyah, with the support of Meshulam and the Levite Shabtay.
\p
\v 16 So the exiled Israelites agreed to follow that plan, and the names of the men were written down by each clan leader who then stayed behind with the priest Ezra, and a few days later, they sat down to start working through the lists.
\v 17 Some two months later, they had finished considering all the men who had married foreign women.
\s1 The men married to foreigners
\p
\v 18 Some of the priests’ descendants had married foreign women, i.e., the descendants of Yotsadak’s son Yeshua and his brothers, Ma’aseyah, Eliezer, Yariv, and Gedalyah,
\v 19 and they promised to expel their wives, and because of their guilt, each of them sacrificed a ram from their flock.
\v 20 Then, \add also\add*:
\li1 From Imer’s descendants: Hanani and Zevadyah,
\li1
\v 21 From Harim’s descendants: Ma’aseyah, Elijah, Shemayah, Yehiel, and Uzziah,
\li1
\v 22 From Pashhur’s descendants: Elyehoeynay, Ma’aseyah, Ishmael, Netanel, Yozavad, and Eleasah,
\li1
\v 23 Among the \add other\add* Levites: Yozavad, and Shimei, and Kelayah (that is, Kelita), Petahyah, Judah, and Eliezer.
\li1
\v 24 Among the singers: Elyashiv.
\li1 Among the \add temple\add* guards: Shalum, Telem, and Uri.
\p
\v 25 From the other Israelites:
\li1 From Par’osh’s descendants: Rameyah, Yiziyah, Malkiyah, Miyamin, Eleazar, Malkiyah, and Benayah.
\li1
\v 26 From Heylam’s descendants: Matanyah, Zechariah, Yehiel, Avdi, Yerimot, and Elijah.
\li1
\v 27 From Zatu’s descendants: Elyehoeynay, Elyashiv, Matanyah, Yerimot, Zavad, and Aziza.
\li1
\v 28 From Bevay’s descendants: Yehohanan, Hananyah, Zabay, and Atlay.
\li1
\v 29 From Bani’s descendants: Meshulam, Maluk, Adayah, Yashuv, and Sheal-Yerimot.
\li1
\v 30 From Pahat-Moab’s descendants: Adna, Kelal, Benayah, Ma’aseyah, Matanyah, Betsalel, Binuy, and Manasseh.
\li1
\v 31 From Harim’s descendants: Eliezer, Yishiyah, Malkiyah, Shemayah, Simeon,
\v 32 Benjamin, Maluk, and Shemaryah.
\li1
\v 33 From Hashum’s descendants: Matenay, Matatah, Zavad, Elifelet, Yeremay, Manasseh, and Shimei.
\li1
\v 34 From Bani’s descendants: Ma’aday, Amram, Uel,
\v 35 Benayah, Bedeyah, Keluhayo,
\v 36 Vanyah, Meremot, Elyashiv,
\v 37 Matanyah, Matenay, and Ya’asu.
\li1
\v 38 From Binuy’s descendants: Shimei,
\v 39 Shelemyah, Nathan, Adayah,
\v 40 Maknadvay, Shashay, Sharay,
\v 41 Azarel, Shelemyah, Shemaryah,
\v 42 Shalum, Amaryah, and Joseph.
\li1
\v 43 From Nevo’s descendants: Yeiel, Matityah, Zavad, Zevina, Yido, Joel, and Benayah.
\p
\v 44 All of those had married foreign women and had children \add from them\add*.
